AB  - Interpretable modelling frameworks are increasingly needed to assess ecological resilience under global change. This study develops an integrated framework coupling the Patch-generating Land Use Simulation (PLUS) model with explainable machine learning (XGBoost-SHAP) to simulate and interpret spatiotemporal ecological resilience patterns based on a Resistance-Adaptability-Recovery (RAR) structure. Applied to eastern Jilin Province, China, it analyses resilience dynamics from 2000 to 2020 and projects conditions to 2035 under multiple SSP-RCP scenarios. Results show a persistent southeast-northwest resilience gradient, with higher resilience in forested uplands. Land-use intensity (LUI) is the dominant intrinsic driver, exhibiting a nonlinear threshold effect. Future projections indicate overall improvement by 2035, strongest under the sustainability-oriented SSP1-2.6 scenario, with dominant drivers shifting from land-use structure to climatic and topographic controls. This framework links resilience projection, driver attribution, and spatial differentiation, offering a methodological tool for resilience-oriented land-use planning under global change.
