AB  - The Soil and Water Assessment Tool (SWAT) is widely used for watershed modelling and water resources management. However, robust SWAT applications often require comprehensive uncertainty analysis, calibration, and optimization, which necessitate the integration of various methods. These workflows typically involve thousands of model runs and the complex manipulation of distributed parameter files, posing challenges to operational feasibility and computational efficiency. This study presents SWAT-UQ, an open-source platform designed for the uncertainty analysis, calibration, and optimization of SWAT models. SWAT-UQ enables the flexible configuration of algorithms to address both uncertainty quantification and decision-oriented tasks. It features a queue-based distributed computing framework that dynamically schedules large ensembles of SWAT runs and intensive file operations. To accommodate diverse user groups, SWAT-UQ is provided as both a scriptable development package and a graphical interface for end-to-end workflows without the need for coding. Two case studies, involving model calibration and Best Management Practice (BMP) multi-objective optimization, demonstrate SWAT-UQ's workflow flexibility, computational efficiency, and accessibility.
