AB  - AutoICE is an automated tool for estimating glacier ice thickness and volume in mountainous regions using remote sensing inputs such as DEMs, glacier outlines, cross-sections, surface velocities, and temperature data. It implements the newly developed Velocity-based Ice thiCkness Estimation (VoICE) model, which applies the shallow ice approximation and ice flow laws to estimate thickness without requiring field measurements. Validation exercise on four glaciers located in High Mountain Asia showed good agreement, although error variability was observed depending on the glacier characteristics. Comparison with in-situ measurements confirmed that the outputs are physically consistent. Likewise, comparisons with existing velocity-based models demonstrated that the proposed VoICE model achieved lower errors and minimal bias in the distributed ice thickness distribution and bed profiles. Additionally, scalability of the AutoICE was demonstrated using 2023 data from the Miyar Basin, western Himalayas. The tool successfully modelled ice thickness, volume, associated uncertainties, and bed topography for 54 glaciers, estimating a total ice volume of 14.20 ± 4.13 km3. Simulations revealed that the processing time ranged from just 30 min to a few hours per glacier, depending on glacier size, on standard computing system, making it practical for both individual glacier studies and large regional-scale applications.
